  <section id="chto-eto">
    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>Inside the brain there are cavities — the <strong>ventricles</strong>. Cerebrospinal fluid is constantly produced in them; it bathes the brain and is reabsorbed. If outflow is blocked or the fluid is poorly absorbed, it accumulates, the ventricles enlarge and compress the brain. This is hydrocephalus.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Obstructive</strong> — outflow is blocked by an obstruction: a tumour, a cyst, narrowing of the cerebral aqueduct.</li>
      <li><strong>Communicating</strong> — the fluid is poorly absorbed, for example after a haemorrhage or meningitis.</li>
      <li><strong>Normal pressure hydrocephalus</strong> — a special form in older people that develops slowly and often goes unrecognised.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>It is important to distinguish hydrocephalus from <strong>atrophy</strong> — a reduction in brain volume where fluid simply fills the freed space. That condition is not treated with surgery.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="simptomy">
    <h2>Symptoms</h2>
    <p>With acute hydrocephalus — severe headache, nausea and vomiting, vision problems, drowsiness. With normal pressure hydrocephalus in older people — a classic combination:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>an unsteady, shuffling gait</strong> — "feet stuck to the floor";</li>
      <li><strong>worsening memory and attention</strong>;</li>
      <li><strong>loss of bladder control</strong>.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If you notice this combination in yourself or your relatives, do not put it down to age — it is a reason for an MRI and a neurosurgeon's review.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="diagnostika">
    <h2>Diagnosis</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Brain MRI</strong> with assessment of the fluid spaces — shows ventricle size, the cause of obstructed outflow, and distinguishes hydrocephalus from atrophy.</li>
      <li><strong>CT</strong> — in acute situations and for follow-up after surgery.</li>
      <li>If normal pressure hydrocephalus is suspected — a <strong>lumbar tap test</strong> removing fluid: if gait clearly improves afterwards, surgery is likely to help.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><a href={`${tg}/179`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">A short overview of hydrocephalus in cards</a> is on my Telegram channel (in Russian).</p>
  </section>

  <section id="lechenie">
    <h2>Treatment</h2>
    <h3>Shunting</h3>
    <p>A thin tube with a valve is placed in a brain ventricle; under the skin it drains excess fluid into the abdominal cavity, where it is absorbed. This is <strong>ventriculoperitoneal shunting</strong> — the main treatment for communicating and normal pressure hydrocephalus. The valve regulates outflow so fluid does not drain too fast.</p>
    <h3>Endoscopic third ventriculostomy (ETV)</h3>
    <p>Through a small opening in the skull an endoscope is passed into the ventricle and a new route for fluid outflow is created — bypassing the obstruction. No shunt is needed. The method suits obstructive hydrocephalus.</p>
    <p>After surgery, follow-up and control imaging are needed.</p>
  </section>

